<section class="knowledge" id="knowledge"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">Indian Knowledge Systems</div><h2>Knowledge in every stone</h2><p>Explore four interconnected areas of traditional knowledge reflected in fort planning and construction.</p></div><div class="knowledge-grid">
<div class="knowledge-card"><img class="knowledge-photo" src="https://media.insider.in/image/upload/w_800/v1768213886/zfknfvk75yoonhldpzh5.jpg" alt="Maha Darwaja, the stone gateway at Raigad Fort" loading="lazy" data-blank-guard="1"><small class="photo-credit">Stone gateway at Raigad Â· Photo: <a href="https://www.district.in/events/raigad-fort-buy-tickets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">District.in</a></small><div class="symbol">ðŸ°</div><h3>Architecture</h3><p>Fort walls, gateways, bastions and watchtowers were shaped by defensive needs and the natural contours of the land. Locally available stone and building techniques were important.</p></div>
<div class="knowledge-card"><img class="knowledge-photo" src="https://images.prismic.io/indiahike/30320-Torna-Fort-Shiva-Ganga-Indiahikes-Nitesh-Kumar.jpg?auto=compress%2Cformat&amp;h=675&amp;rect=0%2C0%2C1920%2C1080&amp;w=1200" alt="Rock-cut water cistern at Torna Fort" loading="lazy" data-blank-guard="1"><small class="photo-credit">Rock-cut cistern at Torna Fort Â· Photo: <a href="https://indiahikes.com/documented-trek/torna-fort-tek" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Indiahikes</a></small><div class="symbol">ðŸ’§</div><h3>Water management</h3><p>Rock-cut cisterns, tanks, ponds and reservoirs helped collect and store rainwater. Their placement and capacity supported people through dry periods and sieges.</p></div>
<div class="knowledge-card"><img class="knowledge-photo" src="https://d3pc1xvrcw35tl.cloudfront.net/images/1200x900/fort556_2026031658702.jpg" alt="Fort walls following a green Sahyadri ridge" loading="lazy" data-blank-guard="1"><small class="photo-credit">Hilltop fort and Sahyadri landscape Â· Photo: <a href="https://www.lokmat.com/maharashtra/maharashtra-budget-announcement-of-schemes-in-honor-of-saints-and-great-men-development-of-forts-as-well-a-a607/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lokmat</a></small><div class="symbol">â›°ï¸</div><h3>Geography</h3><p>Hilltops, ridgelines, cliffs, passes and coastlines influenced fort locations. Elevation and natural barriers offered visibility, access control and protection.</p></div>
<div class="knowledge-card"><small class="photo-credit">Forest-covered fort gateway Â· Photo: <a href="https://mindtrip.ai/attraction/raigad-maharashtra/avchitgad-fort/at-Qe92Yf23" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mindtrip</a></small><div class="symbol">ðŸŒ¿</div><h3>Environment</h3><p>Local stone, rainfall patterns, slopes and vegetation shaped construction and daily life. Studying these choices offers lessons in adapting to local conditions.</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="timeline"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">A glimpse through time</div><h2>Layers of history</h2><p>Maharashtraâ€™s forts were built, expanded and used across many centuries by different powers and communities.</p></div><div class="timeline-list">
<div class="era"><strong>EARLY PERIOD</strong><h3>Hill strongholds</h3><p>Rock-cut caves, routes and elevated settlements show the long history of using the Deccan landscape.</p></div>
<div class="era"><strong>MEDIEVAL PERIOD</strong><h3>Fort networks</h3><p>Regional dynasties developed and maintained strategic forts, routes and fortified settlements.</p></div>
<div class="era"><strong>17TH CENTURY</strong><h3>Swarajya &amp; naval power</h3><p>Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj strengthened many forts and developed a strategic coastal defence network.</p></div>
<div class="era"><strong>MODERN DAY</strong><h3>Heritage &amp; conservation</h3><p>Forts are valued as cultural landscapes that need responsible visitation, documentation and preservation.</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="explore" id="quiz"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">Learn by exploring</div><h2>Think like a fort planner</h2><p>Test your understanding and reflect on the traditional knowledge behind these remarkable places.</p></div><div class="explore-grid"><div class="panel"><h3>Why build a water tank in rock?</h3><p>Imagine a fort located high on a hill, far from a river. Which approach would best help its residents manage water?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button>A. Depend only on water carried from the village</button><button>B. Collect monsoon rainwater in rock-cut cisterns</button><button>C. Build the fort only near a waterfall</button></div><div class="quiz-result" id="quizResult" aria-live="polite"></div></div><div class="panel"><h3>Observe. Understand. Preserve.</h3><p>When visiting a fort, look beyond the views. Notice how the structure fits its landscape.</p><ul><li>Look for cisterns, tanks and channels that collect rainwater.</li><li>Observe how walls and gateways follow the shape of the terrain.</li><li>Notice local stone, vegetation and signs of weathering.</li><li>Do not litter, write on walls or damage historic structures.</li></ul></div></div></section>
